@meta
id: 04-04
title: Counting sideways
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: hjkl, count-prefix
par: 17
minutes: 4
vscode: double-click the word and retype it
open: customer.entity.ts

@brief
`l` moves right one character and takes a count like everything else, so
`11l` moves eleven characters right. `h` goes back the same way, which is
what you use when you overshoot by one or two.

The optional address field is spelled `Adress`. The type it refers to,
`Address`, is spelled correctly — leave that one alone. Put the missing
`d` in, so the field reads `defaultAddress`.

Columns are counted from where the cursor is, and the cursor starts at
column 1 of line 1.

@keys
| `11l` | right eleven characters |
| `2h` | left two characters |
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |

@hints
Get to the right line first, then worry about the column. It is the fifth
line of the file.
--
Count the characters from the start of the line up to the letter the `d`
has to go in front of — the two leading spaces count.
--
`4j`, then `11l` puts you on the `r` of `Adress`, then `i` and type `d`.
If you land one character out, `h` or `l` fixes it before you insert.

@solution
    4j 11l i d <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes, and most of the thinking was counting to eleven.

Which is the honest weakness of counting columns: it is exact, but you
have to look at the characters one by one to get the number, and a long
line makes that silly. Nobody types `47l`. Chapters 5 and 6 give you `w`
to move by words and `fA` to jump straight to the next capital `A`, and
those are what you will really use sideways.

Counts on `l` and `h` stay useful for the short ones — nudging two or
three characters into position, or backing up after an overshoot. Counts
on `j` and `k`, which you met last lesson, are the ones you will use all
day.

@start customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
  defaultAdress?: Address;
};

@expected customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
  defaultAddress?: Address;
};
